{"question": "A type of fatal adrenal cancer has an average survival rate of 3 years after the onset of clinically significant symptoms. It is determined that patients have a 4-year asymptomatic period prior to developing clinical symptoms from this adrenal cancer. A new screening test is developed, and the cancer can now be detected 2 years prior to developing clinical symptoms. A new observational case-control study of screened and unscreened patients measures the median 5-year survival of patients who decline medical and surgical treatment. The screened population has statistically better outcomes. Which step should be undertaken to prevent bias in these conclusions?", "options": {"A": "Using survival rates in the analysis", "B": "Using mortality rates in the analysis", "C": "Analyzing the results by age of diagnosis", "D": "Increasing the number of screened individuals", "E": "Decreasing the number of screened individuals"}, "pred_answer": "E", "gold_answer": "B", "question_domains": ["Oncology", "Endocrinology", "Epidemiology", "Surgery", "Internal Medicine"], "option_domains": ["Epidemiology", "Biostatistics"], "question_analyses": {"Oncology": "The medical scenario describes a rare and fatal adrenal cancer with specific characteristics and a new screening test that allows for earlier detection. Here's a detailed analysis of the scenario:\n\n### Interpretation of the Condition\n1. **Nature of the Cancer**: The cancer is described as fatal with an average survival rate of 3 years after the onset of clinically significant symptoms. This indicates a highly aggressive form of cancer.\n2. **Asymptomatic Period**: Patients have a 4-year asymptomatic period before developing clinical symptoms. This long period without symptoms suggests that the cancer grows slowly or is not detectable by current methods during this time.\n3. **New Screening Test**: The development of a new screening test that can detect the cancer 2 years prior to the onset of clinical symptoms. This